::ALG2_U01_L02_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L02_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2; MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify (3x<sup>2</sup> + 2x - 5) + (x<sup>2</sup> - 7x + 9). {
=4x<sup>2</sup> - 5x + 4 #Correct. Combine like terms: 3x squared plus x squared is 4x squared, 2x minus 7x is -5x, and -5 plus 9 is 4.
~4x<sup>2</sup> + 9x + 4 #This adds 2x and 7x instead of combining 2x and -7x.
~3x<sup>4</sup> - 5x + 4 #This incorrectly adds exponents when adding like terms. Exponents stay the same when like terms are combined.
~4x<sup>2</sup> - 5x - 14 #This combines the variable terms correctly but multiplies the constants instead of adding them.
}

::ALG2_U01_L02_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L02_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2; MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify (5x<sup>2</sup> - 3x + 8) - (2x<sup>2</sup> + x - 6). {
~3x<sup>2</sup> - 2x + 2 #This does not distribute the subtraction sign to every term in the second polynomial.
=3x<sup>2</sup> - 4x + 14 #Correct. Distribute the subtraction sign to get 5x squared - 3x + 8 - 2x squared - x + 6, then combine like terms.
~7x<sup>2</sup> - 2x + 2 #This adds the x squared terms and does not subtract the second polynomial.
~3x<sup>2</sup> + 4x + 14 #The x-term sign is incorrect. -3x - x equals -4x.
}

::ALG2_U01_L02_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L02_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2; MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 3x(2x<sup>2</sup> - 5x + 4). {
~6x<sup>2</sup> - 15x + 12 #This multiplies coefficients but does not add exponents when multiplying by x.
=6x<sup>3</sup> - 15x<sup>2</sup> + 12x #Correct. Distribute 3x to each term and add exponents for powers with the same base.
~6x<sup>3</sup> - 5x<sup>2</sup> + 4x #This does not multiply every coefficient by 3.
~6x<sup>3</sup> + 15x<sup>2</sup> + 12x #The middle term should be negative because 3x times -5x is -15x squared.
}

::ALG2_U01_L02_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L02_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2; MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify (x + 4)(x - 6). {
~x<sup>2</sup> - 24 #This multiplies only the first and last terms and misses the middle products.
~x<sup>2</sup> + 10x - 24 #This uses +6x instead of -6x when multiplying x by -6.
=x<sup>2</sup> - 2x - 24 #Correct. x squared - 6x + 4x - 24 combines to x squared - 2x - 24.
~x<sup>2</sup> + 2x - 24 #The middle terms should combine to -2x, not +2x.
}

::ALG2_U01_L02_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L02_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.2; MLA.A2.AE.4<br><br>Question:<br>A rectangle has length x + 3 and width x + 5. What polynomial represents the area? {
~2x + 8 #This adds length and width, but area requires multiplication.
~x<sup>2</sup> + 15 #This multiplies only the first and last terms and misses the middle terms.
=x<sup>2</sup> + 8x + 15 #Correct. Area is (x + 3)(x + 5), which equals x squared + 8x + 15.
~x<sup>2</sup> - 8x + 15 #The middle term should be positive because both binomial constants are positive.
}
